1
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:22,177
The company that should not exist was born in
April, in a lobby with a living wall.

2
00:00:22,746 --> 00:00:28,566
Legally, it was a merger. Umbrella Research Division
Seven, of Delaware, and Verdant Axis

3
00:00:28,586 --> 00:00:34,143
Therapeutics, of Delaware and the NASDAQ, became a
single corporation called Umbrella

4
00:00:34,163 --> 00:00:39,916
Life Sciences, Incorporated, trading under the ticker
U-M-B-R. The minority shareholders

5
00:00:39,936 --> 00:00:45,493
who had not tendered were bought out at the offer
price. The green letters were gone.

6
00:00:45,513 --> 00:00:51,463
The lawsuit from Graham Tolliver's lawyers was
withdrawn, because Graham Tolliver's lawyers

7
00:00:51,483 --> 00:00:53,956
now worked, technically, for Umbrella.

8
00:00:54,527 --> 00:01:00,023
And on a Monday morning, the hundred and forty
people who had worked for Verdant Axis

9
00:01:00,043 --> 00:01:05,862
gathered in their own atrium, beneath their own living
wall, to meet the man who now owned

10
00:01:05,882 --> 00:01:06,186
them.

11
00:01:06,756 --> 00:01:12,161
They were frightened. They had read the newspaper.
They had watched their company collapse

12
00:01:12,181 --> 00:01:17,406
in a day, and their chief executive walk out of the
building with nothing in his hands,

13
00:01:17,426 --> 00:01:22,650
and they had heard — because everybody had heard —
that the new man had done it without

14
00:01:22,670 --> 00:01:23,736
raising his voice.

15
00:01:25,305 --> 00:01:26,939
He did not give a sermon.

16
00:01:27,510 --> 00:01:33,112
That was a decision, and it had been argued about,
in the car, on the drive across the

17
00:01:33,132 --> 00:01:33,505
river.

18
00:01:34,195 --> 00:01:40,030
A hundred and forty. Frightened. Leaderless. You will
never have a better audience. Give

19
00:01:40,050 --> 00:01:42,293
them what you gave the break room.

20
00:01:42,813 --> 00:01:48,390
The break room was six people who'd already been saved.
These people have just been conquered.

21
00:01:48,410 --> 00:01:52,559
You don't preach to the conquered on the
first day. You reassure them.

22
00:01:53,078 --> 00:01:57,175
You make them grateful first. And then
you make them believe.

23
00:01:57,695 --> 00:02:00,960
I make them productive first.
Belief is expensive.

24
00:02:02,281 --> 00:02:06,544
So he spoke for ninety seconds, and he
said exactly four things.

25
00:02:07,233 --> 00:02:12,771
Nothing you did in this building was wrong, except
believing Graham Tolliver. Nobody here

26
00:02:12,791 --> 00:02:18,577
will lose their job this year. The work that was good
will continue, under better leadership,

27
00:02:18,597 --> 00:02:21,886
with more money. And the work that
was a lie is over.

28
00:02:22,887 --> 00:02:28,174
That's all. Go back to work. Your new chief financial
officer will be in touch about your

29
00:02:28,194 --> 00:02:30,201
salaries. I think you'll like her.

30
00:02:31,722 --> 00:02:36,907
They went back to work. They were not converted.
They were relieved, which is a smaller

31
00:02:36,927 --> 00:02:38,643
thing, and a more useful one.

32
00:02:39,212 --> 00:02:41,411
The System paid him for it anyway.

33
00:02:42,731 --> 00:02:45,570
Assessment. Corporate integration.

34
00:02:49,016 --> 00:02:52,687
It pays for relief. How disappointing.

35
00:02:53,207 --> 00:02:55,256
It pays for order. You should approve.

36
00:02:57,276 --> 00:03:02,112
While Catherine built the company that the world
could see, Mister Pell built the one

37
00:03:02,132 --> 00:03:02,854
it could not.

38
00:03:03,425 --> 00:03:09,576
He did it in the spring of twenty twenty-seven, in his
windowless office on the old building's

39
00:03:09,596 --> 00:03:15,420
second floor, with a cheese sandwich and three
monitors and a pen he never seemed to need

40
00:03:15,440 --> 00:03:16,077
to refill.

41
00:03:16,646 --> 00:03:22,853
A holding company in Nevada, where the owners of a
corporation do not have to be named.

42
00:03:22,873 --> 00:03:29,509
A research subsidiary in Ireland, where the tax on
intellectual property is kind. A logistics

43
00:03:29,529 --> 00:03:35,878
company in Singapore called Ashford Pacific, which
would one day move a great many things

44
00:03:35,898 --> 00:03:42,319
that did not appear on any manifest. A property
company in Wilmington that began, quietly,

45
00:03:42,339 --> 00:03:48,760
to exercise the right of first refusal in section nine
of a contract Dale Kuchar had never

46
00:03:48,780 --> 00:03:55,129
read, and bought two more lots in the industrial
park, and then a third that had not been

47
00:03:55,149 --> 00:03:56,776
in the contract at all.

48
00:03:57,346 --> 00:04:03,172
Beneath the clean public company, like the roots of
a tree beneath a lawn, the shells

49
00:04:03,192 --> 00:04:09,295
went down into four jurisdictions, and joined, and
branched, and joined again, until even

50
00:04:09,315 --> 00:04:14,798
Catherine Adeyemi, who was very good at this,
could only see the top two layers.

51
00:04:15,367 --> 00:04:17,208
That was, of course, the point.

52
00:04:19,276 --> 00:04:24,047
The Arklay sublevel had arrived on the night
of the ten-pull, as promised.

53
00:04:24,617 --> 00:04:30,356
Nobody had heard it arrive. There had been no
construction, no excavation, no trucks.

54
00:04:30,376 --> 00:04:36,453
Gus had simply come to the Director's office the next
morning and said there was a problem

55
00:04:36,473 --> 00:04:39,706
with the freight elevator — it
had a new button.

56
00:04:40,276 --> 00:04:45,828
The freight elevator in the basement of the old
building had gone to two floors for forty

57
00:04:45,848 --> 00:04:51,150
years: the loading dock and the basement. Now,
below the basement button, there was a

58
00:04:51,170 --> 00:04:56,659
third. It had no label. It was simply a black button
with a thin crimson ring around it,

59
00:04:56,679 --> 00:05:02,169
and when you pressed it, the elevator went down for a
great deal longer than the geology

60
00:05:02,189 --> 00:05:05,300
of the Christina River valley
should have allowed.

61
00:05:06,870 --> 00:05:08,843
The doors opened onto white.

62
00:05:09,413 --> 00:05:15,154
A corridor, three hundred feet long, clean enough
to operate in, lit by panels in the

63
00:05:15,174 --> 00:05:21,119
ceiling that gave off a cold, shadowless light. Glass
walls on either side. Behind them,

64
00:05:21,139 --> 00:05:26,949
laboratories: benches, hoods, sequencers, cold
rooms, airlocks, pressure suits hanging

65
00:05:26,969 --> 00:05:32,982
on racks like the skins of astronauts. At the far
end, a steel door six inches thick with

66
00:05:33,002 --> 00:05:38,812
a painted symbol on it that had been, in another
world, the most feared logo on Earth.

67
00:05:39,382 --> 00:05:45,403
It was a containment facility of the highest grade —
the kind of laboratory where, anywhere

68
00:05:45,423 --> 00:05:51,246
else on Earth, you would work on the diseases that
had no cure. There were perhaps sixty

69
00:05:51,266 --> 00:05:57,287
such facilities in the world. Every one of them was
known, licensed, inspected, and mapped.

70
00:05:57,858 --> 00:06:03,868
This one was not. It did not exist. It did not appear
on a single drawing or permit, and

71
00:06:03,888 --> 00:06:09,694
it did not show on ground-penetrating radar, and
the city of Wilmington had no record

72
00:06:09,714 --> 00:06:15,725
of it. It was simply there, beneath the building,
beneath the basement, beneath the grey

73
00:06:15,745 --> 00:06:18,808
machine, the way a thought is
beneath a face.

74
00:06:20,577 --> 00:06:25,877
He walked it alone on the first morning, from one
end to the other, and did not say a

75
00:06:25,897 --> 00:06:26,189
word.

76
00:06:26,759 --> 00:06:32,431
In his old world, he had walked corridors like this
beneath a mansion in the Arklay Mountains,

77
00:06:32,451 --> 00:06:37,941
when he was young and brilliant and still thought he
was the cleverest man in the building.

78
00:06:37,961 --> 00:06:43,390
He had been wrong then. There had always been someone
above him — some old man with money,

79
00:06:43,410 --> 00:06:48,536
some board, some founder. Here, there was no one.
For the first time in either of his

80
00:06:48,556 --> 00:06:51,019
lives, there was no one at all above him.

81
00:06:51,709 --> 00:06:52,926
A fortress.

82
00:06:53,446 --> 00:06:54,493
A laboratory.

83
00:06:55,012 --> 00:07:00,432
Every fortress is a laboratory. It is where
you find out what men will do.

84
00:07:02,452 --> 00:07:08,798
They moved the Tyrant down on the third night. Gus
and two of the Silent Ones, and a set

85
00:07:08,818 --> 00:07:14,007
of heavy-duty machine dollies that Gus had,
as usual, simply always had.

86
00:07:14,576 --> 00:07:20,162
The cradle breathed the whole way down in the
freight elevator, a long slow cycle, and

87
00:07:20,182 --> 00:07:25,767
when they rolled it into the cold room at the end of
the corridor and bolted it to the

88
00:07:25,787 --> 00:07:31,373
floor, it seemed — Gus would have said, if Gus ever
said things like that — to settle.

89
00:07:31,393 --> 00:07:33,393
As if it knew it had come home.

90
00:07:35,463 --> 00:07:37,954
The Silent Ones had arrived the same week.

91
00:07:38,524 --> 00:07:44,073
There were six of them, as promised. Men of the same
height and build, in the same black

92
00:07:44,093 --> 00:07:49,516
suits, with the same short hair and the same
pleasant, forgettable faces. They took up

93
00:07:49,536 --> 00:07:55,149
posts around the old building and the tower with no
instructions that anyone heard given.

94
00:07:55,169 --> 00:08:00,655
They did not go to lunch. They did not seem to talk
to one another, and yet they always

95
00:08:00,675 --> 00:08:02,997
seemed to know where the others were.

96
00:08:03,567 --> 00:08:05,564
And cameras did not like them.

97
00:08:06,134 --> 00:08:11,830
It was subtle. It was not invisibility — nothing so
theatrical. It was simply that on

98
00:08:11,850 --> 00:08:17,682
security footage, a Silent One tended to be slightly
wrong. A little blurred, even when

99
00:08:17,702 --> 00:08:23,869
he was standing still. A little smeared, as if he had
moved between frames. Face-recognition

100
00:08:23,889 --> 00:08:29,721
software, pointed at one of them, returned a
confidence score of nearly zero, and then,

101
00:08:29,741 --> 00:08:31,200
occasionally, crashed.

102
00:08:32,770 --> 00:08:35,225
Walter Pruitt noticed on the fourth night.

103
00:08:35,796 --> 00:08:41,560
He was in the booth, at ten past three, doing the
thing that night guards do and nobody

104
00:08:41,580 --> 00:08:47,211
else ever does — actually watching the cameras. And
on the lobby feed, one of the new

105
00:08:47,231 --> 00:08:52,862
men in black walked from the elevator to the door,
and for the whole of the walk, his

106
00:08:52,882 --> 00:08:53,993
face was a smear.

107
00:08:54,562 --> 00:08:58,937
Walter rewound it. He watched it again.
He watched it six times.

108
00:08:59,506 --> 00:09:05,017
Then he took a small spiral notebook out of his
breast pocket — the kind he had carried

109
00:09:05,037 --> 00:09:10,484
since the Army, to write down things that were none
of his business — and he wrote the

110
00:09:10,504 --> 00:09:12,710
date, and the time, and four words.

111
00:09:13,279 --> 00:09:14,682
Camera doesn't see him.

112
00:09:16,251 --> 00:09:22,308
He said nothing to anyone. That was also a habit from
the Army. But he kept writing things

113
00:09:22,328 --> 00:09:28,317
in the notebook, over the next two years, whenever he
saw something he could not explain.

114
00:09:28,337 --> 00:09:34,394
And the notebook would outlive him, and end up, one
day, in the hands of exactly the wrong

115
00:09:34,414 --> 00:09:34,866
person.

116
00:09:36,937 --> 00:09:40,969
Catherine Adeyemi had asked for three
things. She got two.

117
00:09:41,538 --> 00:09:47,174
She became chief financial officer on the first of
April. She received her equity, on

118
00:09:47,194 --> 00:09:53,762
terms Pell drafted and she tore up and redrafted twice,
and which were, in the end, extraordinarily

119
00:09:53,782 --> 00:09:59,684
generous. She took an office on the eleventh floor of
the tower with a view of the river,

120
00:09:59,704 --> 00:10:05,473
and hired a team of eight from Verdant's old finance
department, and within a month she

121
00:10:05,493 --> 00:10:11,262
had rebuilt the public accounts of Umbrella Life
Sciences into something she could have

122
00:10:11,282 --> 00:10:14,722
shown to a regulator in London with
a straight face.

123
00:10:15,293 --> 00:10:20,555
The third thing — where the money actually went — she
believed she had as well. That was

124
00:10:20,575 --> 00:10:21,576
the beauty of it.

125
00:10:22,144 --> 00:10:28,434
In May she found a line in the consolidated capital
budget that she did not recognise.

126
00:10:28,454 --> 00:10:34,669
Arklay: cold archive infrastructure. Eleven million
dollars, capitalised, depreciated

127
00:10:34,689 --> 00:10:40,024
over twenty years, booked through a Nevada
subsidiary she had not set up.

128
00:10:40,595 --> 00:10:42,324
She went to the Director with it.

129
00:10:43,514 --> 00:10:44,689
What's Arklay?

130
00:10:45,208 --> 00:10:50,743
The sample vault. The archive — the thing that paid
for all of this. It needs better cold

131
00:10:50,763 --> 00:10:56,423
storage than a basement full of freezers from the
nineteen-nineties. Pell set it up through

132
00:10:56,443 --> 00:11:00,355
Nevada for the tax treatment. You can
see it any time you like.

133
00:11:00,875 --> 00:11:02,326
I'd like to see it now.

134
00:11:03,447 --> 00:11:08,776
He took her down in the freight elevator — to the
basement, and not one button further.

135
00:11:08,796 --> 00:11:14,372
He showed her the old freezers, and six new ones, and
a gleaming cold vault Pell had bought

136
00:11:14,392 --> 00:11:19,906
from the Shop for eighteen hundred Points that very
morning, specifically so that it would

137
00:11:19,926 --> 00:11:25,441
be there to show her. He showed her the invoices. He
showed her the depreciation schedule.

138
00:11:25,461 --> 00:11:28,023
Every single thing he showed her was real.

139
00:11:28,595 --> 00:11:34,401
The grey machine was gone from the corner. There was
only a clean patch of concrete, and

140
00:11:34,421 --> 00:11:37,249
four bolt holes, and a faint
smell of cold.

141
00:11:37,937 --> 00:11:38,913
All right.

142
00:11:39,433 --> 00:11:40,202
All right?

143
00:11:40,724 --> 00:11:43,666
All right. Thank you. I don't
enjoy asking twice.

144
00:11:44,186 --> 00:11:45,361
You'll never have to.

145
00:11:47,081 --> 00:11:53,111
It was the first lie he told her. It would not be
the last. But it was, in its way, the

146
00:11:53,131 --> 00:11:59,023
best one — because it was made entirely of true
things, arranged in an order that did

147
00:11:59,043 --> 00:12:00,693
not add up to the truth.

148
00:12:01,383 --> 00:12:03,099
You enjoyed that.

149
00:12:03,619 --> 00:12:09,295
I enjoy craftsmanship. She's the only person in the
building worth lying to properly.

150
00:12:11,315 --> 00:12:13,545
There were other promotions that spring.

151
00:12:14,115 --> 00:12:20,208
Evelyn Marsh became head of the assay programme, with
twelve of Verdant's scientists reporting

152
00:12:20,228 --> 00:12:25,931
to her, and moved into a lab on the ninth floor of
the tower, and was seen there, by the

153
00:12:25,951 --> 00:12:28,858
cleaners, at every hour of the
day and night.

154
00:12:29,427 --> 00:12:35,255
Tomás Reyes became head of digital, and was given a
budget, and did not yet know what

155
00:12:35,275 --> 00:12:36,288
to spend it on.

156
00:12:36,856 --> 00:12:43,577
And Doctor Nadia Okafor was made director of virology —
the best-equipped virology laboratory

157
00:12:43,597 --> 00:12:49,593
she had ever worked in, with a staff of twenty,
and a salary she had to read twice.

158
00:12:50,164 --> 00:12:54,240
She took it. She would think about that
decision for the rest of her life.

159
00:12:54,811 --> 00:13:00,646
She took it because the work was real, and good, and
she was the best person in the building

160
00:13:00,666 --> 00:13:06,120
to do it. She took it because she had a mother in
Lagos who needed an operation, and a

161
00:13:06,140 --> 00:13:11,594
niece at university in Manchester. And she took it
because some part of her — the part

162
00:13:11,614 --> 00:13:16,305
that had not clapped in the break room —
wanted to be close enough to see.

163
00:13:17,874 --> 00:13:23,163
From her new office on the eighth floor, if she
stood at the window, she could see the

164
00:13:23,183 --> 00:13:28,473
old brick building across the river. At night, she
noticed, the lights in its basement

165
00:13:28,493 --> 00:13:29,399
never went out.

166
00:13:29,970 --> 00:13:33,653
She did not know yet that there was
anything beneath the basement.

167
00:13:34,224 --> 00:13:39,465
But she was a virologist. Her whole career had been
spent looking for things that were

168
00:13:39,485 --> 00:13:44,788
invisible, and she had learned, long ago, to trust
the feeling of something being there

169
00:13:44,808 --> 00:13:46,257
before she could see it.
